    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "First, let's recall the properties of the complex number \\( i \\) raised to different integer powers:\n\\[\ni^1 = i, \\quad i^2 = -1, \\quad i^3 = -i, \\quad i^4 = 1, \\quad i^5 = i, \\quad i^6 = -1, \\ldots\n\\]\nThis sequence repeats every 4 values. Therefore, for any integer \\( k \\), \\( i^k \\) can be reduced modulo 4 to one of \\( i, -1, -i, 1 \\).\n\nWe are asked to find the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number. For \\(i^x + i^y\\) to be real, the imaginary parts of \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) must cancel each other out. This happens when \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) are purely imaginary or their imaginary parts are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n\nThe imaginary parts of \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) are \\( \\pm i \\) or \\( \\pm i \\). Specifically:\n- \\(i^x\\) is purely imaginary for \\(x \\equiv 1, 3 \\pmod{4}\\).\n- \\(i^y\\) is purely imaginary for \\(y \\equiv 1, 3 \\pmod{4}\\).\n\nFor \\(i^x + i^y\\) to be real, we need \\(x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\) and \\(y \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\) (or vice versa). This means that the \\(i\\) terms in \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) cancel out when added together.\n\nFirst, we count the number of integers \\(x\\) in the range \\(1 \\le x \\le 100\\) such that \\(x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\). These numbers are \\(1, 5, 9, \\ldots, 97\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term 1 and common difference 4. The number of terms in this sequence is:\n\\[\n\\frac{97 - 1}{4} + 1 = 25\n\\]\nNext, we count the number of integers \\(y\\) in the range \\(1 \\le y \\le 100\\) such that \\(y \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\). These numbers are \\(3, 7, 11, \\ldots, 99\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term 3 and common difference 4. The number of terms in this sequence is:\n\\[\n\\frac{99 - 3}{4} + 1 = 25\n\\]\nTherefore, the total number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) is the product of the number of choices for \\(x\\) and the number of choices for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n25 \\times 25 = 625\n\\]\nThus, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number is \\(\\boxed{625}\\).",
