      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number. Let's start by examining the powers of the imaginary unit \\(i\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^1 &= i, \\\\\ni^2 &= -1, \\\\\ni^3 &= -i, \\\\\ni^4 &= 1, \\\\\ni^5 &= i, \\\\\ni^6 &= -1, \\\\\n&\\vdots\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe observe that the powers of \\(i\\) repeat every 4 terms. Specifically, \\(i^x\\) follows the cycle \\(i, -1, -i, 1\\). For \\(i^x + i^y\\) to be a real number, the imaginary parts of \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) must cancel each other out. This happens when:\n\n\\[\ni^x + i^y = i^y + (-i^x) = (i^y - i^x)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\ni^x = i^y \\quad \\text{or} \\quad i^x = -i^y\n\\]\n\nLet's consider these cases separately.\n\n### Case 1: \\(i^x = i^y\\)\n\nIf \\(i^x = i^y\\), then \\(x \\equiv y \\pmod{4}\\). Since \\(x < y\\), \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must be of the form \\(4k\\) and \\(4k+1\\) for some integer \\(k\\).\n\n### Case 2: \\(i^x = -i^y\\)\n\nIf \\(i^x = -i^y\\), then \\(i^x = i^{y+4}\\). This implies \\(x \\equiv y+4 \\pmod{4}\\), or \\(x \\equiv y+1 \\pmod{4}\\). Since \\(x < y\\), we have \\(x = y + 4k\\) with \\(k > 0\\).\n\nNow, we need to count the number of valid pairs \\((x, y)\\) for each case.\n\n#### Case 1: \\(x \\equiv y \\pmod{4}\\)\n\nFor each valid \\(x\\), there are \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{100}{4} \\right\\rfloor = 25\\) possible values for \\(y = x + 4k\\). Since \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\), \\(k\\) ranges from 1 to 24. For each \\(x\\), there are 24 valid \\(y\\) values. Therefore, the total number of pairs is:\n\n\\[\n25 \\times 24 = 600\n\\]\n\n#### Case 2: \\(x \\equiv y+1 \\pmod{4}\\)\n\nFor each valid \\(x\\), there are \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{100}{4} \\right\\rfloor = 25\\) possible values for \\(y = x + 4k + 1\\). Since \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\), \\(k\\) ranges from 1 to 24. For each \\(x\\), there are 25 valid \\(y\\) values. Therefore, the total number of pairs is:\n\n\\[\n25 \\times 25 = 625\n\\]\n\nAdding the number of pairs from both cases, we get:\n\n\\[\n600 + 625 = 1225\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1225}\n\\]",
