    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\leq x < y \\leq 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number, we first need to understand the behavior of the powers of the imaginary unit \\(i\\).\n\nRecall that the powers of \\(i\\) cycle every 4 terms:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^1 &= i, \\\\\ni^2 &= -1, \\\\\ni^3 &= -i, \\\\\ni^4 &= 1, \\\\\ni^5 &= i, \\\\\ni^6 &= -1, \\\\\n&\\vdots\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus, \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) will both be real numbers if and only if the exponents \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are congruent to 0 or 2 modulo 4. This is because \\(i^0 = 1\\) and \\(i^2 = -1\\), and these are the only values of \\(i^x\\) that are real numbers.\n\nWe need to count the number of pairs \\((x, y)\\) where both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are congruent to 0 or 2 modulo 4. Let's denote the number of integers from 1 to 100 that are congruent to 0 or 2 modulo 4 by \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) respectively.\n\nTo find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we count the numbers:\n- The numbers congruent to 0 modulo 4 are \\(4, 8, 12, \\ldots, 100\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term 4 and common difference 4. The number of terms is:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{100 - 4}{4} + 1 = 25.\n  \\]\n- The numbers congruent to 2 modulo 4 are \\(2, 6, 10, \\ldots, 98\\). This is an arithmetic sequence with the first term 2 and common difference 4. The number of terms is:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{98 - 2}{4} + 1 = 25.\n  \\]\nThus, \\(a = 25\\) and \\(b = 25\\).\n\nThe number of pairs \\((x, y)\\) where both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are congruent to 0 or 2 modulo 4 is the product of the number of choices for \\(x\\) and the number of choices for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n25 \\times 25 = 625.\n\\]\nTherefore, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\leq x < y \\leq 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number is \\(\\boxed{625}\\).",
